Bundled audio asset
-------------------

assets/bingbingbing.ogg — and the same payload embedded in lib/client.js — is a
short clip that was cut, levelled and trimmed (one 1.06 s round, 48 kHz mono Ogg
Vorbis, 12 642 bytes) by this project from a sound effect that circulates as a
public internet meme (the looping "bing bing bing" tone). The clip is **not**
original work of this project's author, and no license is granted for it here.

  * It is included so the plugin works out of the box on the author's machine
    and for other users who want the same familiar tone.
  * If you are the rights holder and want it removed, open an issue and it will
    be replaced with a silent placeholder in the next release.
  * If you redistribute this package, or use it commercially, replace the asset
    with one you have the rights to:

      powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File tools\embed-audio.ps1 -Source C:\path\to\your-tone.ogg

    The plugin then carries your tone and none of the above applies. The settings
    page also lets any end user upload their own tone at runtime, which is the
    recommended path when distribution rights are unclear.

Everything else in `lib/` (the completion detector, the audio player, the
notice layer, the settings page, the host-half schema and diagnostics route) was
written for this package.

The synthesized tones
---------------------

`assets/crisp-a.ogg` and `assets/crisp-b.ogg` (and their payloads embedded in
`lib/client.js`) are **original additive synthesis**, generated by
`tools/synthesize_tones.py` in this repository. No third-party audio, sample
library or recording is included, so no third-party rights apply to them.

They are modelled on *measured facts* about two well-known notification sounds,
not on copies of them. The references were read with `tools`-style analysis
scripts (spectrum, harmonic product spectrum, envelope) during development; no
reference material is redistributed in this package or in this repository.

    crisp-a — two ascending notes (F#6 then F#7), the lower one short and the
              upper one long, onsets ~135 ms apart. Measured from reference
              material: two notes an octave apart, ~80 ms and ~430 ms at the
              strike, total under a second.
    crisp-b — three ascending marimba notes (D4 -> A4 -> D5) as straight eighth
              notes. The instrument and the phrasing come from the sound's
              documented history (Kelly Jacklin's "158-marimba", 1999: a marimba
              patch on a Yamaha XG module, three ascending scale degrees, no
              fancy timing). Measuring the recognisable version shows those three
              fundamentals with the 3rd and 5th partials prominent and decaying
              faster than the fundamental, which is what the synthesiser models.

Both are deliberately *not* transcriptions: the pitches, spacing and decay were
chosen from the measurements and from musical judgement, and the timbre is
synthesized from scratch. If you want the exact sound of a device you own, use
the settings page's **custom tone** upload — that keeps the audio on your machine
and out of any redistribution.
